<span class="kicker">2026-07-22 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Clean a Gravel Driveway or Patio</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Cleaning a gravel driveway or patio isn’t like pressure washing concrete or pavers. It’s mainly about raking, regrading, and removing organic buildup, since loose gravel isn’t meant to be power-washed. The goal is less about “washing” and more about keeping the gravel even, well-drained, and free of the debris that builds up over time.</p>
<p>At<a href="/about/"> Ridgewell Landscape and Design</a>, we install and maintain gravel driveways and patios across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and surrounding communities. Here’s what actually keeps a gravel surface looking clean and functioning well long-term.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Question</b></td><td><b>Answer</b></td></tr><tr><td>Can you pressure wash gravel?</td><td>No, pressure washing isn’t for loose gravel (only attached stone borders)</td></tr><tr><td>Main cleaning method?</td><td>Raking and regrading</td></tr><tr><td>Most common gravel problems?</td><td>Migrating gravel, organic buildup, ruts/potholes</td></tr><tr><td>Best long-term fix?</td><td>Weed barrier underneath and periodic maintenance</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Why Gravel Cleaning Is Different From Other Surfaces</b></h2>
<p>With concrete or pavers, cleaning usually means pressure washing away stains and buildup from a solid surface. Gravel doesn’t work that way. Since it’s loose material, high-pressure water can actually wash gravel out of place rather than clean it, making problems worse instead of better. That’s a very different maintenance approach than what we recommend for a solid surface, where our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-clean-a-paver-patio/"> how to clean a paver patio</a> covers safe pressure washing techniques for pavers specifically.</p>
<p>Pressure washing does have a role, but only for attached stone borders or edging, not the loose gravel itself. For the gravel surface, maintenance is really about managing three things: organic debris, evenness, and drainage. Get those three right and a gravel driveway or patio can look sharp and function well for decades with relatively little effort, even though it never looks or behaves quite like a solid paved surface.</p>
<h2><b>The Most Common Gravel Driveway and Patio Problems</b></h2>
<p>Before getting into maintenance steps, it helps to know what actually causes gravel surfaces to look messy or uneven over time. Almost every issue we see on gravel driveways and patios across the Denver area traces back to one of three root causes.</p>
<h3><b>Gravel Migrating Out of the Area</b></h3>
<p>Without proper edging, gravel naturally spreads over time, pushed by tires, foot traffic, or water runoff, leaving thin or bare spots and gravel scattered onto surrounding grass or walkways. This is one of the most common complaints we hear, and it’s almost always an edging problem rather than a gravel problem. Homeowners often assume they need more gravel, when really the fix is containing what’s already there.</p>
<h3><b>Organic Buildup</b></h3>
<p>Leaves, dirt, and plant debris settle into gravel over seasons, mixing in and making the surface look dirty even when the gravel itself is fine underneath. In Colorado, this tends to be worse in fall and after spring runoff, when organic material gets carried into the gravel by wind and water. Left unaddressed for a full season or two, this buildup can start to hold moisture against the gravel and encourage weed growth in spots that were previously clean.</p>
<h3><b>Ruts, Potholes, and Uneven Settling</b></h3>
<p>High-traffic areas, especially on driveways, gradually develop low spots and ruts as gravel compresses unevenly or shifts under repeated weight. Tire paths are usually the worst offenders, since the same spots take the same pressure trip after trip. On sloped driveways, this problem is often made worse by water runoff carving small channels along the same low points where vehicles already travel.</p>
<h2><b>How to Actually Clean and Maintain a Gravel Driveway or Patio</b></h2>
<h3><b>Rake and Regrade Regularly</b></h3>
<p>This is the core maintenance step. Raking redistributes gravel that’s drifted out of place and helps break up compacted or rutted areas, keeping the surface level and even. For driveways, this usually means working a rake or a rear-mounted grading attachment across the tire paths where compaction and rutting are worst. For patios, a simple hand rake every few weeks is often enough to keep the surface tidy and looking intentional rather than neglected.</p>
<h3><b>Remove Organic Debris</b></h3>
<p>Periodically clearing leaves, dirt, and plant matter keeps the gravel looking clean and prevents buildup that can trap moisture and encourage weed growth. A leaf blower works well for lighter debris, while a rake is better for anything that’s started to settle into the gravel itself. Doing this a couple of times each fall, and again after any major spring runoff, keeps buildup from accumulating to the point where it becomes a bigger job.</p>
<h3><b>Install a Weed Barrier Underneath</b></h3>
<p>If your gravel driveway or patio doesn’t already have a fabric barrier underneath, adding one helps prevent weeds from pushing through and reduces how much organic material mixes into the gravel from below. This is one of the biggest long-term differences between a gravel surface that stays clean-looking for years and one that constantly needs attention no matter how often it’s raked.</p>
<h3><b>Top Off With Fresh Gravel as Needed</b></h3>
<p>Over time, gravel naturally settles and thins out in high-traffic spots. Adding fresh gravel periodically keeps depth and coverage consistent, and it’s a far simpler fix than letting thin spots turn into ruts or bare patches of exposed soil that then need to be regraded and refilled properly.</p>
<h3><b>Use Pressure Washing Only Where Appropriate</b></h3>
<p>If your gravel area has stone borders, edging, or a patio section with fixed pavers, pressure washing works well there, just not on the loose gravel itself. Keeping borders clean also makes the whole space read as more intentional and well-kept, even if the gravel field itself is doing exactly what it’s supposed to do.</p>

