<h2><b>A Simple Maintenance Checklist</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Task</b></td><td><b>Frequency</b></td></tr><tr><td>Rake/regrade gravel</td><td>Every few months, or as ruts appear</td></tr><tr><td>Remove leaves/debris</td><td>Seasonally, especially fall</td></tr><tr><td>Check for weed growth</td><td>Ongoing, more often in spring/summer</td></tr><tr><td>Top off gravel</td><td>As needed, typically every 1 to 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure wash borders/edging</td><td>As needed for visible staining</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Why a Weed Barrier Makes a Real Difference</b></h2>
<p>A lot of gravel maintenance headaches trace back to what’s, or isn’t, underneath the gravel. Without a proper weed barrier, weeds root directly into the ground beneath the gravel and push through, and organic material from the soil below mixes upward into the gravel over time. Installing a barrier during setup (or retrofitting one) cuts down significantly on both weed growth and the “dirty” look that comes from organic buildup.</p>
<p>This is very similar to how base preparation works for other hardscape surfaces. Just as<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> proper base depth</a> determines whether a paver patio holds up long-term, what’s underneath a gravel surface determines how much ongoing maintenance it needs. Skipping this step during installation is one of the most common reasons a gravel driveway or patio starts looking messy again just a year or two after it was put in, even when the homeowner is doing everything right on the surface.</p>
<h2><b>Why Edging Matters as Much as the Gravel Itself</b></h2>
<p>It’s easy to focus entirely on the gravel and overlook the border that contains it, but edging does a huge amount of the structural work in keeping a gravel surface looking clean. Steel, aluminum, timber, or a stone or paver border all serve the same basic purpose: creating a defined line that keeps gravel from spreading into the lawn, garden beds, or walkways.</p>
<p>Without solid edging, even a well-raked, freshly topped gravel driveway will slowly spread outward, especially in areas that see regular vehicle traffic or water runoff after storms. If your gravel area has been drifting for years despite regular maintenance, the underlying issue is often the absence of proper edging rather than anything you’re doing wrong with cleaning. Adding edging retroactively is usually a straightforward fix and one of the highest-impact upgrades for a tired-looking gravel surface.</p>
<h2><b>Gravel vs. Other Driveway and Patio Materials</b></h2>
<p>Gravel is popular in Colorado for good reason. It drains well, handles freeze-thaw cycles without cracking the way concrete can, and it’s typically less expensive to install than pavers or poured concrete. That said, it does trade off some of the low-maintenance convenience that comes with a solid surface like<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> interlocking pavers</a>, which don’t require raking or regrading at all.</p>
<p>If you’re weighing gravel against a paver driveway or patio, it’s worth reading through our comparison on<a href="/blog/can-patio-pavers-be-used-for-a-driveway/"> whether patio pavers can be used for a driveway</a>, which walks through how paver surfaces perform differently under vehicle weight and what that means for long-term upkeep. Some homeowners land on a hybrid approach, using gravel for a secondary parking area or a low-traffic path, and pavers for the main driveway or entertaining patio, which balances cost with reduced maintenance in the areas that get the most use.</p>
<h2><b>Seasonal Considerations for Colorado Gravel Surfaces</b></h2>
<p>Colorado’s climate adds a few specific wrinkles to gravel maintenance that are worth planning around. Spring runoff, especially on sloped driveways, can wash gravel downhill or into low spots faster than normal wear alone would. Regrading right after the spring thaw is one of the most effective single maintenance tasks you can do each year, and it sets the surface up to handle the rest of the year with far less drifting.</p>
<p>Fall brings the heaviest organic debris load, particularly if your property has deciduous trees nearby, which makes seasonal debris removal especially important going into winter. And winter itself can be tough on gravel edges if snow plowing or shoveling regularly pushes gravel past its borders, another reason solid, well-installed edging pays off over time rather than being treated as a cosmetic extra.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Can I pressure wash my gravel driveway?</b></h3>
<p>Not the loose gravel itself, the water pressure can displace it. Pressure washing works well on any attached stone borders or edging, just not the gravel surface.</p>
<h3><b>How often should I rake a gravel driveway?</b></h3>
<p>Every few months is a good starting point, or whenever you notice ruts, uneven spots, or gravel that’s drifted out of the main area.</p>
<h3><b>Why does my gravel driveway keep developing ruts?</b></h3>
<p>Repeated vehicle traffic gradually compresses and shifts gravel unevenly, especially in the same tire paths. Regular raking and regrading helps prevent ruts from becoming permanent.</p>
<h3><b>Do I need a weed barrier under gravel?</b></h3>
<p>It’s strongly recommended. A weed barrier reduces weed growth and helps keep organic material from the soil below from mixing into the gravel over time.</p>
<h3><b>How do I stop gravel from spreading onto my lawn or walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Proper edging along the border of the gravel area is the most effective way to keep it contained and prevent it from migrating outward over time.</p>

